1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:02,472
Picture an ordinary hospital.

2
00:00:02,472 --> 00:00:05,861
A baby boy is born, and his parents are Jewish.

3
00:00:05,861 --> 00:00:13,477
The next day, a non-Jewish doctor offers to circumcise him right there: quick, sterile, under medical supervision.

4
00:00:13,477 --> 00:00:14,701
The parents agree.

5
00:00:15,056 --> 00:00:21,063
Years later, the family learns that according to Jewish law, this circumcision might not count.

6
00:00:21,063 --> 00:00:22,145
How can that be?

7
00:00:22,145 --> 00:00:27,300
The foreskin is gone, the operation was done, physically everything is in place.

8
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:30,264
Why would anyone say the commandment was not fulfilled?

9
00:00:30,642 --> 00:00:34,123
And if it was not fulfilled, what now: cut again?

10
00:00:34,123 --> 00:00:36,030
This is not a made-up scenario.

11
00:00:36,030 --> 00:00:46,043
In the twentieth century, especially in the Soviet Union, thousands of Jewish boys were circumcised by doctors in hospitals, and many were never circumcised at all.

12
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:51,099
When these people began returning to tradition, the question became very real.

13
00:00:51,099 --> 00:00:58,638
Today we are going to work through it properly: from the Book of Genesis to the Talmud, from Maimonides to the Shulchan Aruch.

14
00:00:59,006 --> 00:01:05,541
And to make the logic of the law truly visible, we will write it as JavaScript code and run it right on screen.

15
00:01:05,541 --> 00:01:10,670
So, the question of the day: does circumcision count if a non-Jew performs it?

16
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:13,546
Let's start at the beginning.

17
00:01:13,546 --> 00:01:19,654
In Hebrew, circumcision is called brit milah, literally, the covenant of circumcision.

18
00:01:19,654 --> 00:01:23,727
It is not just a medical procedure, and it is not a hygiene custom.

19
00:01:24,102 --> 00:01:38,730
In chapter seventeen of Genesis, God makes a covenant with Abraham and says: This is My covenant, which you shall keep, between Me and you and your descendants after you: every male among you shall be circumcised.

20
00:01:39,088 --> 00:01:45,855
And then: at eight days old, every male among you shall be circumcised, throughout your generations.

21
00:01:45,855 --> 00:01:51,744
Abraham circumcised himself at the age of ninety nine, and his son Isaac on the eighth day.

22
00:01:52,106 --> 00:02:02,739
Since then, for nearly four thousand years, Jews all over the world have done this on the eighth day of a child's life, even when that day falls on Shabbat or on Yom Kippur.

23
00:02:02,739 --> 00:02:04,749
Notice one word: covenant.

24
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:10,076
The Torah describes circumcision not as a result, but as the sign of a bond.

25
00:02:10,076 --> 00:02:12,911
And that is exactly where our whole question comes from.

26
00:02:12,911 --> 00:02:17,228
If only the result matters, then it does not matter who holds the knife.

27
00:02:17,614 --> 00:02:23,530
But if it is an act inside a covenant, then perhaps it matters a great deal who performs it, and why.

28
00:02:24,988 --> 00:02:29,848
Before asking who may perform a circumcision, let's understand what it consists of.

29
00:02:29,848 --> 00:02:34,874
In halacha, that is, Jewish law, brit milah has several stages.

30
00:02:34,874 --> 00:02:38,546
The first is milah, the actual removal of the foreskin.

31
00:02:38,918 --> 00:02:45,440
The second is periah, folding back the thin inner membrane so that the glans is fully exposed.

32
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:50,479
Without periah, says the Mishnah, it is as if the circumcision never happened.

33
00:02:50,479 --> 00:02:54,628
The third stage is metzitzah, drawing the blood from the wound.

34
00:02:55,008 --> 00:03:02,110
This is more of a medical and traditional element, and how it should be done today is debated, but that is a separate topic.

35
00:03:02,110 --> 00:03:04,095
And finally, the blessings.

36
00:03:04,450 --> 00:03:14,761
The father thanks God for commanding him to bring his son into the covenant of Abraham, and the mohel, the person who performs the circumcision, says his own blessing.

37
00:03:14,761 --> 00:03:17,248
Let's write this as a JavaScript structure.

38
00:03:17,612 --> 00:03:23,206
You can see that each step has a field saying whether it is required for the circumcision to be valid.

39
00:03:23,206 --> 00:03:28,013
If even one required step is missing, the check function returns false.

40
00:03:28,013 --> 00:03:30,732
Remember this structure, we will need it again.

41
00:03:32,210 --> 00:03:36,232
Now for the main question: who is allowed to perform a circumcision?

42
00:03:36,232 --> 00:03:42,624
In tractate Avodah Zarah, page twenty seven, the Talmud begins with a very broad rule.

43
00:03:42,624 --> 00:03:44,828
Everyone is fit to circumcise.

44
00:03:44,828 --> 00:03:47,753
And then comes a surprising example: a woman.

45
00:03:48,132 --> 00:03:50,801
Where do we see that a woman may circumcise?

46
00:03:50,801 --> 00:03:55,131
People usually point to the story of Tzipporah, the wife of Moses.

47
00:03:55,131 --> 00:04:02,798
In chapter four of Exodus, on the way to Egypt, Moses is in mortal danger because their son has not been circumcised.

48
00:04:03,166 --> 00:04:07,601
And Tzipporah takes a sharp stone and circumcises her son herself.

49
00:04:07,601 --> 00:04:09,121
And the danger passes.

50
00:04:09,121 --> 00:04:15,655
Maimonides and the Shulchan Aruch do indeed include a woman in the list of those fit to circumcise.

51
00:04:16,016 --> 00:04:22,680
So neither being male, nor any special rank, nor rabbinic status is required for the commandment itself.

52
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:27,319
Of course, in practice circumcision is done by an experienced mohel.

53
00:04:27,319 --> 00:04:32,242
That is a matter of the child's health, and today it is always a trained specialist.

54
00:04:32,610 --> 00:04:38,578
But from the point of view of pure law, the circle of people who can perform the commandment is very wide.

55
00:04:38,578 --> 00:04:40,150
So where is its boundary?

56
00:04:40,150 --> 00:04:41,645
Does it include a non-Jew?

57
00:04:41,645 --> 00:04:46,993
Here the Talmud opens a debate, and this is the most interesting part of our analysis.

58
00:04:48,456 --> 00:04:51,628
So, Avodah Zarah, page twenty seven.

59
00:04:51,628 --> 00:04:57,401
The Talmud asks: how do we know that a circumcision performed by a non-Jew is invalid?

60
00:04:57,401 --> 00:05:01,331
And it gives two different sources, from two different sages.

61
00:05:01,714 --> 00:05:08,726
The first, Daru bar Pappa in the name of Rav, says: it is written, and you shall keep My covenant.

62
00:05:08,726 --> 00:05:15,208
The meaning is this: a circumcision can be performed by someone who is himself inside the covenant.

63
00:05:15,572 --> 00:05:19,388
Someone outside the covenant cannot bring another person into it.

64
00:05:19,388 --> 00:05:25,522
The second, Rabbi Yochanan, quotes a different verse: he shall surely be circumcised.

65
00:05:25,878 --> 00:05:34,385
In Hebrew, that is himol yimol, and Rabbi Yochanan reads it as a play on words: the one who is circumcised shall circumcise.

66
00:05:34,385 --> 00:05:40,570
In other words, the criterion is not belonging to the people, but the fact of being circumcised.

67
00:05:40,570 --> 00:05:42,117
What difference does it make?

68
00:05:42,496 --> 00:05:45,951
In most cases, both criteria give the same answer.

69
00:05:45,951 --> 00:05:50,307
But the Talmud itself asks: what is the practical difference between them?

70
00:05:50,307 --> 00:05:53,052
And it answers: two interesting cases.

71
00:05:53,052 --> 00:05:58,980
The first case is a circumcised Arab, that is, a non-Jew who is himself circumcised.

72
00:05:59,354 --> 00:06:09,317
The second is a Jew who is not circumcised because his older brothers died from circumcision, so the law forbids him to undergo it, since it is a danger to his life.

73
00:06:09,317 --> 00:06:11,211
Let's see what this looks like in code.

74
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:14,344
We write two functions.

75
00:06:14,344 --> 00:06:20,182
The first follows Rav: a person may circumcise if he is himself inside the covenant.

76
00:06:20,182 --> 00:06:25,684
A Jew is in the covenant, even if for a legitimate reason he is not circumcised.

77
00:06:26,058 --> 00:06:31,704
The second function follows Rabbi Yochanan: a person may circumcise if he is circumcised.

78
00:06:31,704 --> 00:06:36,150
Now we create an array of people and run each of them through both functions.

79
00:06:36,150 --> 00:06:39,887
A circumcised Jewish man: both functions say yes.

80
00:06:39,887 --> 00:06:42,954
An uncircumcised non-Jew: both say no.

81
00:06:43,324 --> 00:06:44,704
No disagreement there.

82
00:06:44,704 --> 00:06:46,354
Now the interesting rows.

83
00:06:46,354 --> 00:06:51,650
A circumcised Arab: according to Rav, no, he is not in the covenant.

84
00:06:51,650 --> 00:06:55,903
According to Rabbi Yochanan, formally yes, he is circumcised.

85
00:06:56,270 --> 00:07:05,627
But in practice the law does not rule this way, and the codes state explicitly: a non-Jew, even a circumcised one, does not circumcise.

86
00:07:05,627 --> 00:07:12,857
Next, a Jew whose brothers died from circumcision: according to Rav, yes, he is in the covenant.

87
00:07:13,224 --> 00:07:17,285
According to Rabbi Yochanan, no, he is not circumcised.

88
00:07:17,285 --> 00:07:18,586
And what about a woman?

89
00:07:18,586 --> 00:07:21,228
The Talmud asks this question itself.

90
00:07:21,228 --> 00:07:29,437
According to Rabbi Yochanan, a woman is considered as if she were circumcised, since she has nothing to circumcise, so she may.

91
00:07:29,818 --> 00:07:32,887
But according to Rav, it comes out that she may not.

92
00:07:32,887 --> 00:07:36,108
And then the Talmud asks: but what about Tzipporah?

93
00:07:36,108 --> 00:07:41,701
And it answers: perhaps she had someone else do it, or she began and Moses finished.

94
00:07:42,068 --> 00:07:48,229
You can see how two short verses become two different functions, and give different answers on the edge cases.

95
00:07:48,229 --> 00:07:52,984
That is Talmudic thinking: find the input on which two logics diverge.

96
00:07:54,458 --> 00:07:58,106
There is a second layer to the debate, and it is about intention.

97
00:07:58,106 --> 00:08:03,815
In the same chapter, the Talmud quotes a baraita, a teaching from the era of the Mishnah.

98
00:08:04,188 --> 00:08:15,066
Rabbi Yehudah says: a Jew may circumcise a Samaritan, but a Samaritan may not circumcise a Jew, because he circumcises in the name of Mount Gerizim.

99
00:08:15,066 --> 00:08:21,251
The Samaritans, or Kutim, held Mount Gerizim to be their holy place, not Jerusalem.

100
00:08:21,622 --> 00:08:27,203
And Rabbi Yehudah is concerned that their circumcision would be dedicated to a different worship.

101
00:08:27,203 --> 00:08:34,278
Rabbi Yosei objects: where do we find in the Torah at all that circumcision requires a special intention?

102
00:08:34,278 --> 00:08:37,255
Let him circumcise, whatever he is thinking about.

103
00:08:37,616 --> 00:08:41,445
So here is another key parameter: kavanah, intention.

104
00:08:41,445 --> 00:08:46,587
Must circumcision be done for the sake of the commandment, or is the act itself enough?

105
00:08:46,587 --> 00:08:53,726
For our question this matters, because a non-Jewish doctor in a hospital is obviously not thinking about any covenant.

106
00:08:54,090 --> 00:08:56,334
He is performing a medical operation.

107
00:08:56,334 --> 00:09:00,458
If intention is required, his work apparently does not count.

108
00:09:00,458 --> 00:09:02,751
If not, then perhaps it does.

109
00:09:02,751 --> 00:09:07,184
Let's add an intent parameter to our code and see how it changes the answer.

110
00:09:08,664 --> 00:09:15,212
Now we move from the Talmud to the codes of law, that is, to how this debate was decided in practice.

111
00:09:15,212 --> 00:09:21,900
We start with Maimonides, the Rambam, the great twelfth century scholar, Rabbi Moshe ben Maimon.

112
00:09:22,258 --> 00:09:37,607
In his code, the Mishneh Torah, in the laws of circumcision, chapter two, he writes: everyone is fit to circumcise, even an uncircumcised man, a slave, a woman, and a minor, where there is no adult man.

113
00:09:37,607 --> 00:09:40,301
But a non-Jew should not circumcise at all.

114
00:09:40,652 --> 00:09:44,983
And if he did circumcise, there is no need to circumcise a second time.

115
00:09:44,983 --> 00:09:50,499
Here two very important concepts of Jewish law appear: lechatchila and bedieved.

116
00:09:50,499 --> 00:09:54,777
Lechatchila means in advance, ideally, how one should act.

117
00:09:55,134 --> 00:09:58,847
Bedieved means after the fact, once it has already been done.

118
00:09:58,847 --> 00:10:03,203
According to the Rambam, lechatchila one may not call a non-Jew.

119
00:10:03,203 --> 00:10:10,316
But bedieved, if the circumcision was already done by a non-Jew, it counts, and nothing needs to be redone.

120
00:10:10,696 --> 00:10:20,839
In code it looks like this: the function returns not just yes or no, but two separate fields: may we do this in advance, and what to do if it is already done.

121
00:10:21,194 --> 00:10:28,824
And by the way, this is very typical of halacha: the same act can be forbidden in advance, yet valid after the fact.

122
00:10:30,296 --> 00:10:38,365
The next stage: the Shulchan Aruch, the code of law compiled by Rabbi Yosef Karo in the sixteenth century in Safed.

123
00:10:38,365 --> 00:10:41,857
To this day it is the main practical code of Jewish law.

124
00:10:42,210 --> 00:10:48,913
In the section Yoreh Deah, chapter two hundred sixty four, he repeats the Rambam almost word for word.

125
00:10:48,913 --> 00:10:56,606
Everyone is fit to circumcise, but a non-Jew, even if he himself is circumcised, should not circumcise at all.

126
00:10:56,956 --> 00:11:00,450
And if he did, there is no need to circumcise again.

127
00:11:00,450 --> 00:11:03,143
But the Shulchan Aruch has a famous addition.

128
00:11:03,518 --> 00:11:17,695
Rabbi Moshe Isserles, known as the Rema, the great rabbi of Krakow, wrote glosses on the text called the Mapah, the tablecloth, because it spreads Ashkenazi customs over the set table, the Shulchan Aruch.

129
00:11:18,072 --> 00:11:24,117
And here the Rema writes: some say that one must again draw the blood of the covenant from the child.

130
00:11:24,117 --> 00:11:31,824
This opinion goes back to early authorities, the Tur cites it, and this is exactly the practice in Ashkenazi communities.

131
00:11:31,824 --> 00:11:33,550
So the picture looks like this.

132
00:11:33,922 --> 00:11:39,091
According to the Shulchan Aruch, circumcision by a non-Jew counts after the fact.

133
00:11:39,091 --> 00:11:44,168
According to the Rema, it does not fully count: an additional procedure is needed.

134
00:11:44,168 --> 00:11:47,635
Let's look at what that procedure is called and what it involves.

135
00:11:49,096 --> 00:11:54,755
This procedure is called hatafat dam brit, literally, a drop of the blood of the covenant.

136
00:11:54,755 --> 00:11:59,110
It is important to understand: nobody cuts the child a second time.

137
00:11:59,110 --> 00:12:04,858
The foreskin is already gone, so repeating the circumcision is physically impossible.

138
00:12:05,210 --> 00:12:12,273
Instead, the mohel makes a tiny prick at the site of the circumcision, so that a single drop of blood appears.

139
00:12:12,273 --> 00:12:13,227
That is all.

140
00:12:13,227 --> 00:12:14,567
It takes seconds.

141
00:12:14,940 --> 00:12:25,238
The point of this act is that the commandment itself is, so to speak, performed again, this time properly, by Jewish hands and for the sake of the covenant.

142
00:12:25,238 --> 00:12:28,434
The same procedure is used in other situations too.

143
00:12:28,798 --> 00:12:33,568
For example, when a man who is already circumcised converts to Judaism.

144
00:12:33,568 --> 00:12:42,614
In tractate Shabbat, page one hundred thirty five, the schools of Shammai and Hillel debate a convert who comes already circumcised.

145
00:12:42,968 --> 00:12:49,864
And in the end, the law recorded in the Shulchan Aruch requires such a convert to undergo hatafat dam brit.

146
00:12:49,864 --> 00:12:56,372
The logic is the same: the body is already circumcised, but the covenant has not yet been made.

147
00:12:56,372 --> 00:12:59,452
So a drop of blood is needed to seal that covenant.

148
00:12:59,802 --> 00:13:05,435
And now it becomes clear why the Rema requires the same after a circumcision by a non-Jew.

149
00:13:05,435 --> 00:13:12,394
The physical result is there, but the act inside the covenant was never performed, and the drop of blood completes it.

150
00:13:13,872 --> 00:13:26,799
Let's put it all together and write one big function, we will call it brit, which takes a description of the situation and returns a ruling according to three authorities: the Rambam, the Shulchan Aruch, and the Rema.

151
00:13:26,799 --> 00:13:28,410
Let's run the first case.

152
00:13:28,762 --> 00:13:32,630
A Jewish mohel, on the eighth day, with the blessings.

153
00:13:32,630 --> 00:13:35,632
Completely clear: valid according to everyone.

154
00:13:35,632 --> 00:13:40,774
The second case: a non-Jewish doctor in a hospital, a medical circumcision.

155
00:13:40,774 --> 00:13:46,431
The Rambam and the Shulchan Aruch: not allowed lechatchila, but bedieved it counts.

156
00:13:46,796 --> 00:13:49,865
The Rema: hatafat dam brit is required.

157
00:13:49,865 --> 00:13:56,321
The third case: a Jewish doctor, not observant at all, performed a circumcision in a clinic.

158
00:13:56,321 --> 00:14:01,385
He is Jewish, so he is in the covenant, and by law the circumcision counts.

159
00:14:01,758 --> 00:14:08,216
Many mohels will still check whether periah was done completely, because medical technique sometimes differs.

160
00:14:08,216 --> 00:14:15,394
The fourth case: a mother circumcised her son herself, like Tzipporah, because no mohel was available.

161
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:21,174
By the basic law this is valid, although many authorities prefer a man lechatchila.

162
00:14:21,174 --> 00:14:27,798
The fifth case: an adult from the former Soviet Union, who was circumcised in a hospital as a child.

163
00:14:28,154 --> 00:14:34,045
According to Ashkenazi practice, he undergoes hatafat dam brit, sometimes as an adult.

164
00:14:34,045 --> 00:14:38,349
And the sixth case: a circumcised Muslim converts to Judaism.

165
00:14:38,349 --> 00:14:41,815
Also hatafat dam brit, but as part of the conversion.

166
00:14:42,204 --> 00:14:48,649
Notice that in every case where the body is already circumcised, the answer is never: cut again.

167
00:14:48,649 --> 00:14:50,994
At most, a single drop of blood.

168
00:14:52,458 --> 00:14:54,869
A few practical notes before we finish.

169
00:14:54,869 --> 00:15:00,436
First: all of this concerns a situation where the circumcision has already been done.

170
00:15:00,436 --> 00:15:09,083
Lechatchila, all authorities agree: you should call a Jew, and ideally an experienced mohel who knows both the law and the medicine.

171
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:14,835
Today many mohels are themselves doctors, and many doctors have trained as mohels.

172
00:15:14,835 --> 00:15:18,018
Second: a non-Jew can absolutely assist.

173
00:15:18,018 --> 00:15:23,907
For example, an anesthesiologist, a nurse, or a pediatrician who examines the baby.

174
00:15:24,278 --> 00:15:29,537
Halacha is concerned with who performs the act itself, not with who is standing nearby.

175
00:15:29,537 --> 00:15:35,220
Third: if you or your child were circumcised in a hospital, there is no need to panic.

176
00:15:35,592 --> 00:15:41,444
This is a very common situation, and there is a simple, long established solution for it.

177
00:15:41,444 --> 00:15:44,885
Speak to the rabbi of your community or to a mohel.

178
00:15:45,250 --> 00:15:53,280
He will check whether periah was done, and tell you whether hatafat dam brit is needed according to your custom, Ashkenazi or Sephardi.

179
00:15:53,280 --> 00:15:57,301
And fourth: this video itself is not a halachic ruling.

180
00:15:57,668 --> 00:16:05,066
We have gone through the sources, but a specific question is always decided by a rabbi who knows all the circumstances.

181
00:16:06,530 --> 00:16:07,678
So, let's sum up.

182
00:16:07,678 --> 00:16:11,609
The question: does circumcision count if a non-Jew performs it?

183
00:16:11,609 --> 00:16:21,184
The Talmud debates where we learn that a non-Jew does not circumcise: from the words about the covenant, or from the words about the one who is himself circumcised.

184
00:16:21,540 --> 00:16:30,240
The Rambam and the Shulchan Aruch say: you may not do this in advance, but if it has already been done, there is no need to repeat it.

185
00:16:30,598 --> 00:16:43,332
And the Rema and the Ashkenazi tradition add: hatafat dam brit must be performed, drawing a drop of the blood of the covenant, so that the circumcision is not only an operation, but also a covenant.

186
00:16:43,332 --> 00:16:46,270
And perhaps that is the main lesson of this whole topic.

187
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:54,129
In Jewish law, what matters is not only the result, but also who performs an act, how, and for what purpose.

188
00:16:54,129 --> 00:17:01,422
Two people can make the very same movement with a knife, and one is surgery, while the other is the covenant of Abraham.

189
00:17:01,794 --> 00:17:03,484
Thank you for watching to the end.

190
00:17:03,484 --> 00:17:09,656
If you found this interesting, tell us in the comments which other questions of Jewish law we should work through with code.

191
00:17:09,656 --> 00:17:10,816
See you next time.
